{
 "project": {
  "title": "长文摘要一致性评测",
  "brief": "三个摘要系统在长文上的一致性评测，结果和笔记都已经有了，要写成能投 AAAI 的完整论文",
  "evidence": [
   {
    "id": "73e04eb9-68d9-41d0-9eda-94886335633e",
    "title": "notes.md",
    "kind": "file",
    "path": ".research/sources/73e04eb9-68d9-41d0-9eda-94886335633e/1.md",
    "sha256": "3ffa7d53485e10f07cd3f520cd600e9aa6d60934478375ceb4b57ee369fd7222",
    "revision": 1,
    "importedAt": "2026-09-23T20:20:26.425Z",
    "coverage": "full-text",
    "verified": true,
    "stale": false,
    "chunks": [
     {
      "text": "# 评测笔记\n\n- 语料：200 篇长文（新闻特稿和机构报告），每个系统每篇生成一段摘要。\n- 三个系统：lead-3（抽取式基线）、bart-large（抽象式）、bart-large-rl（加了一致性奖励的强化学习微调）。\n- 长度分档：按摘要模型的分词器计 token，短（3k 以下）70 篇、中（3k 到 6k）70 篇、长（6k 以上）60 篇。\n- 人工标注：每篇摘要由两名标注者判断是否与原文一致，分歧由第三人裁决；表里是裁决后被判为一致的比例。正式标注前先用语料外的一小批摘要做了校准。\n- 自动指标：SummaC（基于 NLI，卷积版，默认设置）和 QAFactEval（基于问答，官方配置），都读全文，不截断。\n- 观察：两个自动指标给出的系统排序与人工一致，每个长度档都一致；但分数和人工一致率不在同一个尺度上，不能直接比大小。\n- 摘要级别用 AUC 看指标能不能区分一致和不一致的摘要：SummaC 在长文档上掉得明显，QAFactEval 比较稳。长档只有 60 篇，这个差别要谨慎说。\n",
      "locator": {
       "line": 1
      }
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "2e0053ea-f34a-4e1f-a58e-ad3cc83b03fc",
    "kind": "literature",
    "title": "SummaC: Re-Visiting NLI-based Models for Inconsistency Detection in Summarization",
    "path": ".research/sources/2e0053ea-f34a-4e1f-a58e-ad3cc83b03fc/reference.json",
    "sha256": "05899bc40fda4152a5b83cc96d69bbe5419bcdb6f25755558fd58510f2e7e014",
    "revision": 1,
    "importedAt": "2026-09-23T20:20:41.438Z",
    "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.1162/tacl_a_00453",
    "doi": "10.1162/tacl_a_00453",
    "coverage": "abstract",
    "verified": true,
    "stale": false,
    "chunks": [
     {
      "locator": {
       "key": "abstract"
      },
      "text": "We provide a highly effective and light-weight method called SummaCConv that enables NLI models to be successfully used for this task by segmenting documents into sentence units and aggregating scores between pairs of sentences. We furthermore introduce a new benchmark called SummaC (Summary Consistency) which consists of six large inconsistency detection datasets. On this dataset, SummaCConv obtains state-of-the-art results with a balanced accuracy of 74.4%, a 5% improvement compared with prior work."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "3eb20dfd-e5b9-4a5d-9c5f-5f27dd516a3e",
    "kind": "literature",
    "title": "QAFactEval: Improved QA-Based Factual Consistency Evaluation for Summarization",
    "path": ".research/sources/3eb20dfd-e5b9-4a5d-9c5f-5f27dd516a3e/reference.json",
    "sha256": "16cacacb8b6a645d96538e980e2208f1f4ce1f86d03cc3d715f0468d94e8dcaf",
    "revision": 1,
    "importedAt": "2026-09-23T20:20:46.876Z",
    "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2022.naacl-main.187",
    "doi": "10.18653/v1/2022.naacl-main.187",
    "fullTextPath": ".research/sources/3eb20dfd-e5b9-4a5d-9c5f-5f27dd516a3e/fulltext.pdf",
    "coverage": "full-text",
    "verified": true,
    "stale": false,
    "chunks": [
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 1
      },
      "text": "Building\non those insights, we propose an optimized\nmetric, which we call QAFACT EVAL, that\nleads to a 14% average improvement over pre-\nvious QA-based metrics on the SummaC fac-\ntual consistency benchmark, and also outper-\nforms the best-performing entailment-based\nmetric."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 2
      },
      "text": "Based on our insights,\nwe propose an optimized metric, which we call\nQAFACT EVAL , that outperforms the entailment-\nbased metrics of Laban et al. (2021)."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 3
      },
      "text": "FactCC Kryscinski et al. (2020) is a RoBERTa-\nbase model trained on FactCC synthetic data to\ncompute a document-level score, and thus the\nscores need not be aggregated over input sentences."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "7c43c309-79b2-4553-bcb4-583100e0a7e3",
    "kind": "literature",
